From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Implement LockWorkStation() by asking the compositor to lock

LockWorkStation() was a stub returning ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED, so every
Windows program and remote-management tool that locks the workstation --
including after an RMM session ends -- silently did nothing.

On Stained Glass OS the compositor owns input and what is on screen, and is
therefore the only thing that can lock for real (ADR 0009, ADR 0011). Windows
code cannot reach its Unix control socket, since Wine has no AF_UNIX, so this
starts a small native helper, sg-lockctl, that sends LOCK. The helper carries
no authority of its own: the compositor decides from the socket's peer
credentials, and anyone in the session may lock.

The helper is named with a \\?\unix\ path rather than through a drive letter:
administrators often remove the drive mapped to / to hide the Linux
filesystem from Windows programs, and locking must not stop working when they
do.

The return value follows Windows' documented contract exactly: the call is
asynchronous, and nonzero means the lock was initiated, not that it
succeeded. That is also all this code can know -- Wine gives a native child a
process handle that cannot be waited on and has no exit code. The outcome is
recorded in the compositor's audit log.

Forwarded: not-needed
--- a/dlls/user32/user_main.c
+++ b/dlls/user32/user_main.c
@@ -331,9 +331,35 @@
  */
 BOOL WINAPI LockWorkStation(void)
 {
-    TRACE(": stub\n");
-    SetLastError( ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED );
-    return FALSE;
+    /* Stained Glass OS: lock by asking the compositor, which owns input and
+     * what is on screen, and therefore the only thing that can lock for real.
+     *
+     * Windows code cannot open its Unix control socket -- Wine has no AF_UNIX --
+     * so this starts a small native program that does. The \\?\unix\ path
+     * does not depend on a drive letter mapping to /, which administrators
+     * often remove to hide the Linux filesystem from Windows programs. The
+     * helper carries no authority: the compositor checks who is asking from
+     * the socket's peer credentials, and anyone in the session may lock.
+     *
+     * Windows documents this call as asynchronous: nonzero "indicates that the
+     * operation has been initiated. It does not indicate whether the
+     * workstation has been successfully locked." That is also all this can
+     * know. Wine gives a native child a process handle that cannot be waited
+     * on and has no exit code, so the result of the request is not visible
+     * here -- it is visible in the compositor's audit log. */
+    static WCHAR cmdline[] = L"\\\\?\\unix\\usr\\libexec\\stained-glass\\sg-lockctl LOCK";
+    STARTUPINFOW si = { sizeof(si) };
+    PROCESS_INFORMATION pi;
+
+    TRACE( "\n" );
+    if (!CreateProcessW( NULL, cmdline, NULL, NULL, FALSE, CREATE_NO_WINDOW, NULL, NULL, &si, &pi ))
+    {
+        WARN( "cannot run sg-lockctl: %lu\n", GetLastError() );
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+    CloseHandle( pi.hThread );
+    CloseHandle( pi.hProcess );
+    return TRUE;
 }
 
